light_sensor.ino<pre><code>const int LDR_PIN = 39;      // AD2 terminal
const int DARK  = 300;       // raw value when covered
const int LIGHT = 3500;      // raw value under a lamp

void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); }

void loop() {
  int raw = analogRead(LDR_PIN);
  int percent = map(raw, DARK, LIGHT, 0, 100);
  percent = constrain(percent, 0, 100);
  Serial.printf("raw=%d  light=%d %%\n", raw, percent);
  delay(200);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <p></p> <ul> <li> <p><code>map(x, inLo, inHi, outLo, outHi)</code> rescales linearly (integers only).</p> </li> <li> <p><code>constrain(x, lo, hi)</code> clips to the range: needed when the sensor goes beyond your calibration.</p> </li> <li> <p>Calibrate: measure DARK and LIGHT in the real environment first.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Calibration procedure</p> <p>1. Print raw values. 2. Note min and max. 3. Put them in constants. 4. Map.</p>

fade.ino<pre><code>const int LED_PIN  = 18;
const int FREQ_HZ  = 5000;
const int RES_BITS = 8;            // duty 0..255

void setup() {
  ledcAttach(LED_PIN, FREQ_HZ, RES_BITS);  // core 3.x
}

void loop() {
  for (int d = 0; d &lt;= 255; d++) { ledcWrite(LED_PIN, d); delay(4); }
  for (int d = 255; d &gt;= 0; d--) { ledcWrite(LED_PIN, d); delay(4); }
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <p>Core 2.x (old tutorials)</p> <p><code>ledcSetup()</code> + <code>ledcAttachPin()</code>, and <code>ledcWrite()</code> takes a channel.</p> <p></p> <ul> <li> <p>16 channels, any output pin.</p> </li> <li> <p>Duty \(= \text{value} / (2^{\text{res}}-1)\).</p> </li> <li> <p>Higher frequency \(\Rightarrow\) lower max. resolution.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>analogWrite()</code> also works (uses LEDC).</p> </li> </ul> <p>Sources: Migration from Arduino ESP32 Core 2.x to 3.0</p>

dimmer.ino<pre><code>const int POT_PIN = 36;
const int LED_PIN = 22;          // green LED

void setup() {
  ledcAttach(LED_PIN, 5000, 12); // 5 kHz, 12 bit
}

void loop() {
  int raw = analogRead(POT_PIN); // 0..4095
  ledcWrite(LED_PIN, raw);       // duty 0..4095
  delay(10);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>Input range = output range (both 12 bit): no conversion needed!</p> </li> <li> <p>With 8-bit PWM you would write <code>raw / 16</code> or <code>map(raw, 0, 4095, 0, 255)</code>.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Gamma</p> <p>Our eyes are non-linear: the LED seems to reach full brightness at half the pot. Try <code>duty = raw * raw / 4095;</code> for a more natural feel.</p>

servo.ino<pre><code>const int SERVO_PIN = 13;

void writeAngle(int deg) {
  int us = map(deg, 0, 180, 500, 2500);   // pulse width
  uint32_t duty = (uint32_t)us * 65535 / 20000;
  ledcWrite(SERVO_PIN, duty);
}

void setup() {
  ledcAttach(SERVO_PIN, 50, 16);  // 50 Hz = 20 ms, 16 bit
}

void loop() {
  writeAngle(0);   delay(1000);
  writeAngle(90);  delay(1000);
  writeAngle(180); delay(1000);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <p>Servo signal</p> <p>Period 20 ms; pulse 0.5–2.5 ms sets the angle 0–180°.</p> <p>Power</p> <p>Feed the servo from 5 V (VIN), not from 3V3; connect the grounds. The 3.3 V signal is usually accepted.</p> <p>Library alternative: ESP32Servo.</p>

dac_ramp.ino<pre><code>// 8-bit DAC: GPIO25 (DAC1), GPIO26 (DAC2)
void setup() {}

void loop() {
  for (int v = 0; v &lt; 256; v++) {
    dacWrite(25, v);      // 0..255 -&gt; 0..3.3 V
    delayMicroseconds(100);
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>A real voltage, not PWM: audio, reference levels.</p> </li> <li> <p>Only on the original ESP32 (and S2).</p> </li> </ul> touch.ino<pre><code>const int THRESHOLD = 30;

void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); }

void loop() {
  int t = touchRead(T0);      // T0 = GPIO4
  Serial.println(t);           // try the Plotter
  if (t &lt; THRESHOLD) Serial.println("Touched!");
  delay(100);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>Any wire or copper pad becomes a button.</p> </li> <li> <p>ESP32: value drops when touched.</p> </li> <li> <p>Calibrate the threshold per board.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Sources: Espressif: GPIO Matrix and Pin Mux</p>

dac_adc_sine.ino<pre><code>#include &lt;math.h&gt;
const int DAC_PIN = 25;
const int ADC_PIN = 32;
const int N = 250;
const float frequency = 10.0;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  analogReadResolution(12); // ADC: 0..4095
}

void loop() {
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++) {
    float angle = 2.0 * PI * frequency * i / N;
    int dacValue = 128 + 127 * sin(angle);
    dacWrite(DAC_PIN, dacValue);
    int adcValue = analogRead(ADC_PIN);
    int adc8bit = adcValue * 255 / 4095;
    Serial.printf("DAC:%d,ADC:%d\n", dacValue, adc8bit);
    // delay(5);
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>Classic ESP32: jumper GPIO25 (DAC1) to GPIO32 (ADC1) on the same board.</p> </li> <li> <p>These GPIOs are on the DevKit headers; GPIO32 is not the carrier’s AD1 terminal (GPIO36).</p> </li> <li> <p><code>dacWrite</code>: 8-bit voltage command, 0–255.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>analogRead</code>: 12-bit measurement, 0–4095.</p> </li> <li> <p>Rescale ADC to 0–255 to compare the curves. This is not voltage calibration; noise, nonlinearity and clipping can cause differences.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Sources: Espressif DAC API; ADC API; GPIO Matrix and Pin Mux</p>

anatomy.ino<pre><code>/*
  Blink the four LEDs of the course board
  Author: you, date
*/
#include &lt;Arduino.h&gt;        // 1. libraries
const int LED_PINS[] = {23, 22, 21, 4};   // 2. globals
const int DELAY_MS = 300;
void allLeds(bool on) {      // 3. own functions
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {
    digitalWrite(LED_PINS[i], on);
  }
}
void setup() {               // 4. runs once
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {
    pinMode(LED_PINS[i], OUTPUT);
  }
}
void loop() {                // 5. runs forever
  allLeds(true);  delay(DELAY_MS);
  allLeds(false); delay(DELAY_MS);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ol> <li> <p>Comment header: what, who, when.</p> </li> <li> <p>Includes: libraries you use (<code>Arduino.h</code> is added automatically in <code>.ino</code>).</p> </li> <li> <p>Global constants and variables: visible everywhere; pin numbers live here.</p> </li> <li> <p>Your own functions: give a name to a job.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>setup()</code> and <code>loop()</code>.</p> </li> </ol> <p>Copying code from these slides</p> <p>Select, copy, paste in the IDE, then press Ctrl+T (Auto Format).</p>

hello.ino<pre><code>void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);          // UART0 -&gt; USB
  delay(500);
  Serial.println("Hello, ESP32!");
  Serial.printf("CPU: %u MHz, cores: %u\n",
                (unsigned) getCpuFrequencyMhz(),
                (unsigned) ESP.getChipCores());
}

void loop() {
  Serial.printf("Uptime: %lu ms\n", millis());
  delay(1000);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p><code>Serial.begin(115200)</code>: match the baud rate in the Serial Monitor.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>printf</code> is available on ESP32 (not on the Uno).</p> </li> <li> <p><code>ESP</code> object: chip info, free heap, MAC …</p> </li> </ul> <p>Garbage on the monitor?</p> <p>Wrong baud rate, or the boot log at 74880 baud.</p>

dht22.ino<pre><code>#include &lt;DHT.h&gt;

DHT dht(25, DHT22);          // data pin, sensor type

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  dht.begin();
}

void loop() {
  float t = dht.readTemperature();   // Celsius
  float h = dht.readHumidity();      // %
  if (isnan(t) || isnan(h)) {
    Serial.println("DHT read failed");
  } else {
    Serial.printf("T = %.1f C  RH = %.0f %%\n", t, h);
  }
  delay(2000);               // DHT22: max one read per 2 s
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>Library: DHT sensor library by Adafruit (+ Adafruit Unified Sensor).</p> </li> <li> <p>The object <code>dht</code> knows the pin and the sensor type.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>isnan()</code>: the library returns “not a number” when the read fails: always check!</p> </li> </ul> <p>Error you will see</p> <p><code>fatal error: DHT.h: No such file or directory</code> \(\Rightarrow\) the library is not installed.</p> <p>Sources: DHT sensor library; Embedded Programming, lectures 1–6</p>

oled.ino<pre><code>#include &lt;Wire.h&gt;
#include &lt;Adafruit_GFX.h&gt;
#include &lt;Adafruit_SSD1306.h&gt;

Adafruit_SSD1306 display(128, 64, &amp;Wire, -1);   // w, h, bus, no reset pin

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Wire.begin(19, 18);                            // course board I2C
  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, 0x3C)) {
    Serial.println("OLED not found");
    while (true) delay(10);
  }
  display.clearDisplay();
  display.setTextSize(2);
  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
  display.setCursor(0, 0);
  display.print("Hello");
  display.display();               // nothing shows before this call!
}

void loop() {}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>Libraries: Adafruit SSD1306 + Adafruit GFX.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>0x3C</code>: the I<sup>2</sup>C address of the display.</p> </li> <li> <p>Drawing happens in a buffer in RAM; <code>display()</code> sends it to the screen.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>&amp;Wire</code>: the address of the I<sup>2</sup>C object the display uses.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Sources: Adafruit SSD1306 Library; Embedded Programming, lectures 1–6</p>

CourseBoard.h<pre><code>// CourseBoard.h: what the library offers
#pragma once              // include this file only once
#include &lt;Arduino.h&gt;

void boardBegin();                // LEDs + buttons
void setLed(int index, bool on);  // index 0..3
void showBinary(uint8_t value);   // 4 bits on the LEDs
bool keyPressed(int key);         // key 1 or 2
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>The header holds only declarations: the “menu” of the library.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>#pragma once</code> prevents errors when the header is included twice.</p> </li> <li> <p>Comments in the header are the user’s documentation.</p> </li> </ul> CourseBoard.cpp<pre><code>// CourseBoard.cpp: how it works
#include "CourseBoard.h"

// static: only visible inside this file (private)
static const int LEDS[4] = {23, 22, 21, 4};
static const int KEYS[2] = {34, 35};

void boardBegin() {
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) pinMode(LEDS[i], OUTPUT);
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2; i++) pinMode(KEYS[i], INPUT);
}

void setLed(int index, bool on) {
  if (index &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; index &lt; 4) digitalWrite(LEDS[index], on);
}

void showBinary(uint8_t value) {
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) setLed(i, (value &gt;&gt; i) &amp; 1);
}

bool keyPressed(int key) {
  return digitalRead(KEYS[key - 1]) == HIGH;   // active HIGH
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <p>Sources: Writing a Library for Arduino</p>

counter.ino<pre><code>#include "CourseBoard.h"    // our own tab

uint8_t counter = 0;
bool lastKey = false;

void setup() {
  boardBegin();
}

void loop() {
  bool key = keyPressed(1);
  if (key &amp;&amp; !lastKey) {      // new press
    counter++;
    showBinary(counter);      // 0..15 on the LEDs
  }
  lastKey = key;
  delay(20);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>The sketch no longer knows which pins are used, or that the buttons are active HIGH.</p> </li> <li> <p>A board with other pins? Only <code>CourseBoard.cpp</code> changes; every sketch keeps working.</p> </li> <li> <p>The names (<code>boardBegin</code>, <code>showBinary</code>) make <code>loop()</code> readable.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Typical errors</p> <p><code>undefined reference to ‘showBinary’</code>: declared in the <code>.h</code> but not defined in the <code>.cpp</code> (or a typo). <code>CourseBoard.h: No such file</code>: the tab is missing or misspelled.</p>

Blinker.h<pre><code>#pragma once
#include &lt;Arduino.h&gt;
class Blinker {
  public:                                  // what users may call
    Blinker(int pin, unsigned long ms);    // constructor
    void begin();
    void update();                         // call often in loop()
  private:                                 // internal state
    int _pin;
    unsigned long _interval;
    unsigned long _last = 0;
    bool _on = false;
};
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>A class bundles data (pin, interval, state) and functions.</p> </li> <li> <p>Each object has its own data: two LEDs, two speeds, one code.</p> </li> <li> <p>Exactly how <code>DHT dht(25, DHT22); dht.begin();</code> works.</p> </li> </ul> Blinker.cpp<pre><code>#include "Blinker.h"
Blinker::Blinker(int pin, unsigned long ms)
  : _pin(pin), _interval(ms) {}
void Blinker::begin() { pinMode(_pin, OUTPUT); }
void Blinker::update() {               // non-blocking
  if (millis() - _last &gt;= _interval) {
    _last = millis();
    _on = !_on;
    digitalWrite(_pin, _on);
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> blink_two.ino<pre><code>#include "Blinker.h"
Blinker blue(23, 250), red(21, 1000);   // two objects
void setup() { blue.begin(); red.begin(); }
void loop()  { blue.update(); red.update(); }
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <p>Sources: Writing a Library for Arduino</p>

comments.ino<pre><code>// single-line comment: until end of line

/*
  multi-line comment
  for headers and longer explanations
*/

// BAD: repeats the code
i = i + 1;              // add 1 to i

// GOOD: explains the reason
delay(20);   // DHT22 needs 20 ms between reads

// GOOD: units and ranges
const int DRY_LIMIT = 30;   // soil humidity, %
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>Comments are ignored by the compiler; they are for humans (including you in 3 weeks).</p> </li> <li> <p>Explain why, units, limits and assumptions; not what every line obviously does.</p> </li> <li> <p>Every file starts with a header: purpose, author, date, board, wiring.</p> </li> <li> <p>Commenting out code (<code>//</code>) is also a quick debugging tool.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Self-documenting code</p> <p>Good names reduce the need for comments: <code>if (soilHumidity &lt; DRY_LIMIT)</code> needs no explanation.</p> <p>Sources: Embedded Programming, lectures 1–6</p>

scope.ino<pre><code>void loop() {
  int x = 10;
  if (x &gt; 5) {
    int y = 20;
    Serial.println(x);   // OK
    Serial.println(y);   // OK
  }
  Serial.println(x);     // OK
  Serial.println(y);     // ERROR: y not
}                        // declared in this scope
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <p></p> <ul> <li> <p>Braces <code>{ }</code> define a scope.</p> </li> <li> <p>A variable exists from its declaration to the <code>}</code> of its block.</p> </li> <li> <p>Inner blocks can see outer variables, not the other way round.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Sources: Example from lecture 3 Embedded Programming, lectures 1–6</p>

lifetimes.ino<pre><code>int globalCount = 0;          // whole program

void countCalls() {
  int localCount = 0;         // new every call
  static int staticCount = 0; // keeps its value
  globalCount++;
  localCount++;
  staticCount++;
  Serial.printf("g=%d l=%d s=%d\n",
                globalCount, localCount, staticCount);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  countCalls();   // g=1 l=1 s=1
  countCalls();   // g=2 l=1 s=2
  countCalls();   // g=3 l=1 s=3
}
void loop() {}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>Global: declared outside all functions; everyone can read and change it.</p> </li> <li> <p>Local: created when the block starts, destroyed at its end.</p> </li> <li> <p>static local: only visible inside the function, but keeps its value between calls.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Use globals sparingly</p> <p>Any function can change a global: in a big program it becomes hard to know who changed what. Prefer parameters and return values.</p>

