button_pinmode_bug.ino<pre><code>const int LED = 23, ON_BUTTON = 34, OFF_BUTTON = 35;

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ON_BUTTON, INPUT);
  pinMode(ON_BUTTON, INPUT); // copy/paste bug!
  digitalWrite(LED, LOW);
}

void loop() {
  if (digitalRead(ON_BUTTON) == HIGH) {
    digitalWrite(LED, HIGH);
  }
  if (digitalRead(OFF_BUTTON) == HIGH) {
    digitalWrite(LED, LOW);
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>The screenshot in lecture 4 configures the ON button twice.</p> </li> <li> <p>Replace the second occurrence with <code>pinMode(OFF_BUTTON, INPUT)</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Print both input readings and check the GPIO labels against the schematic.</p> </li> <li> <p>Course-board buttons are active HIGH with external pull-downs.</p> </li> </ul> <p>A suspicious line is a hypothesis</p> <p>A pin can already be an input after reset, so this omission alone need not explain the symptom. Test the wiring, input value and executed branch.</p> <p>Sources: Lecture 4, slide 12; pin names adapted to the course board Embedded Programming, lectures 1–6</p>

blink.ino<pre><code>const int LED_PIN = 2;      // on-board LED

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);
  delay(500);                // waits: nothing else runs
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  delay(500);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <p>External LED </p> <p></p> <ul> <li> <p>\(R = (3.3 - V_F)/I \approx (3.3-2.0)/4\,\text{mA} \approx 330\,\Omega\).</p> </li> <li> <p>Pins use the GPIO number, not the label “D2”.</p> </li> <li> <p>During <code>delay()</code>, your sketch waits before executing its next statement.</p> </li> </ul>

four_leds.ino<pre><code>const int LED_BLUE   = 23;
const int LED_GREEN  = 22;
const int LED_RED    = 21;
const int LED_ORANGE = 4;
void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_BLUE, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(LED_GREEN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(LED_RED, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(LED_ORANGE, OUTPUT);
}
void loop() {
  digitalWrite(LED_BLUE, HIGH);   delay(200);
  digitalWrite(LED_GREEN, HIGH);  delay(200);
  digitalWrite(LED_RED, HIGH);    delay(200);
  digitalWrite(LED_ORANGE, HIGH); delay(200);
  digitalWrite(LED_BLUE, LOW);
  digitalWrite(LED_GREEN, LOW);
  digitalWrite(LED_RED, LOW);
  digitalWrite(LED_ORANGE, LOW);
  delay(500);
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>The LEDs light up one by one, then all switch off together.</p> </li> <li> <p>It works, but notice the repetition: 4 almost identical lines, three times.</p> </li> <li> <p>In the chapters Functions and Loops and Arrays we will shrink this to a few lines.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Try</p> <p>Change the order, or the delays, to make a “Knight Rider” effect.</p>

plant_pot.ino<pre><code>const int SENSOR_PIN = 36;     // AD1 terminal (analog)
const int PUMP_PIN   = 26;     // to transistor / relay
const int DRY_LIMIT  = 30;     // percent
int readHumidity() {
  int raw = analogRead(SENSOR_PIN);       // 0..4095
  return map(raw, 4095, 0, 0, 100);       // dry sensor = high raw
}
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(PUMP_PIN, OUTPUT);
}
void loop() {
  int humidity = readHumidity();          // read soil humidity
  if (humidity &lt; DRY_LIMIT) {             // humidity &lt; 30 %?
    digitalWrite(PUMP_PIN, HIGH);         // pump on 10 s
    delay(10000);
    digitalWrite(PUMP_PIN, LOW);
    Serial.printf("Humidity %d %% -&gt; watered\n", humidity);
  } else {
    Serial.printf("Humidity %d %% -&gt; soil OK\n", humidity);
  }
  delay(60000);                           // wait 1 minute
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>Each box became one or a few lines.</p> </li> <li> <p>The decision diamond became an <code>if / else</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>“read soil humidity” became a function: easy to test on its own.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>map()</code> converts the raw ADC value to 0–100 %.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Calibrate!</p> <p>Measure the raw value in dry air and in water first; real sensors never use the full 0–4095 range.</p>

all_leds.ino<pre><code>const int LEDS[] = {23, 22, 21, 4};
void turnOnAllLeds() {
  digitalWrite(23, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(22, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(21, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(4, HIGH);
}
void turnOffAllLeds() {
  digitalWrite(23, LOW);
  digitalWrite(22, LOW);
  digitalWrite(21, LOW);
  digitalWrite(4, LOW);
}
void setup() {
  for (int pin : LEDS) pinMode(pin, OUTPUT);
}
void loop() {
  turnOnAllLeds();
  delay(500);
  turnOffAllLeds();
  delay(500);
}
