ternary.ino<pre><code>const int LED_PIN = 21;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  int temperature = 32;

  // Choose a value with if / else:
  int ledState;
  if (temperature &gt; 30) {
    ledState = HIGH;
  } else {
    ledState = LOW;
  }

  // The same choice with the ternary operator:
  ledState = (temperature &gt; 30) ? HIGH : LOW;
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, ledState);
  Serial.println(temperature &gt; 30 ? "hot" : "OK");
}
void loop() {}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <p>Conditional operator (ternary)</p> <p><code>condition ? a : b</code></p> <ul> <li> <p>Ternary means three operands: one condition and two alternatives.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>true</code>: choose <code>a</code> (after <code>?</code>). <code>false</code>: choose <code>b</code> (after <code>:</code>).</p> </li> <li> <p>Only the selected alternative is evaluated.</p> </li> <li> <p>Produces a value: assign it, return it, or pass it to a function.</p> </li> <li> <p>Try 32: <code>HIGH</code>, prints <code>hot</code>. Try 25: <code>LOW</code>, prints <code>OK</code>.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Keep it readable</p> <p>Prefer <code>if/else</code> for complex choices. Avoid nested ternaries.</p>

