four_timers.ino<pre><code>hw_timer_t *timer1 = nullptr;
hw_timer_t *timer2 = nullptr;
hw_timer_t *timer3 = nullptr;
hw_timer_t *timer4 = nullptr;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  timer1 = timerBegin(1000000);
  timer2 = timerBegin(1000000);
  timer3 = timerBegin(1000000);
  timer4 = timerBegin(1000000);
}

void loop() {}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>In Arduino-ESP32 Core 3.x, you do not manually select Timer 0, 1, 2 or 3. Each <code>timerBegin()</code> allocates an available timer.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>1000000</code> is the tick frequency in Hz, not a timer number: 1 MHz, or 1 \(\mu\)s per tick.</p> </li> <li> <p><code>timer1</code> to <code>timer4</code> are variable names, not hardware timer IDs.</p> </li> <li> <p>This example targets the classic ESP32 with 4 timers; all four must be available. Other ESP32 variants may have fewer.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Allocation is separate from configuring an alarm</p> <p>Check each returned pointer for <code>nullptr</code>: allocation can fail. Successful calls start the counters; no ISR runs until you configure an alarm and attach a handler. <code>timerEnd(timer1)</code> releases that timer for reuse.</p> <p>Sources: Espressif Timer API; Core 3.3.11: timerBegin() implementation</p>

hw_timer.ino<pre><code>hw_timer_t *timer = nullptr;
volatile bool tick = false;
const int LED_PIN = 2;
bool ledOn = false;
void ARDUINO_ISR_ATTR onTimer() { tick = true; }
void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  timer = timerBegin(1000000);           // 1 MHz: 1 tick = 1 us
  timerStop(timer);                     // pause while configuring
  timerAttachInterrupt(timer, &amp;onTimer);
  // 0.5 s alarm, auto-reload, repeat forever:
  timerAlarm(timer, 500000, true, 0);
  timerRestart(timer);                  // reset count to zero
  timerStart(timer);                    // start counting now
}

void loop() {
  if (tick) {
    tick = false;
    ledOn = !ledOn;
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, ledOn);
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>Download this example</p> <ul> <li> <p>4 general-purpose 64-bit timers.</p> </li> <li> <p>Precise, jitter-free periodic events: sampling an ADC at a fixed rate, generating signals.</p> </li> <li> <p>Period \(= \text{alarm} / f_{\text{timer}}\).</p> </li> </ul> <p>API changed in core 3.x</p> <p>Old: <code>timerBegin(0, 80, true)</code> + <code>timerAlarmWrite()</code> + <code>timerAlarmEnable()</code>.</p> <p>Sources: Migration from Arduino ESP32 Core 2.x to 3.0</p>

